## 1.2 The 5-Layer Heritage Twin Concept
The atomic object of Heritage Guard is the **Heritage Twin**. Every site is not a point on Google Maps, but a versioned 5-layer entity:

| Layer | Name | Description | Key Attributes | SIH Significance |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Layer 1** | **Cultural Identity** | Core tangible history, local vernacular names, architectural taxonomy, community origin stories. | Site IDs, vernacular aliases, historical period, patron dynasty, architectural school, public narrative. | Eliminates anglicized-only lookup; honors indigenous designations. |
| **Layer 2** | **Living Practice** | The intangible heritage giving the site its pulse: rituals, artisanal crafts, folklore, seasonal fairs. | Associated craft communities, sacred routes, festival calendar, consent status (`public`, `community-only`, `withheld`). | Meets UNESCO 2003 Convention compliance for community participation. |
| **Layer 3** | **Spatial Truth** | Source-attributed geospatial polygons for protected monument, prohibited zone (100m), regulated zone (200m). | GeoJSON MultiPolygons, source URL (Bhuvan/ASI gazette), datum, capture date, known boundary limitations. | Replaces naive circle approximations with legal/scientific GIS boundaries. |
| **Layer 4** | **Condition & Change** | Chronological record of physical condition, environmental impact, and neutral crowd observations. | Date-stamped observations, concern taxonomy, photo provenance, GPS error margins, non-accusatory notes. | Converts isolated complaints into longitudinal condition trends. |
| **Layer 5** | **Safeguarding Workflow** | Administrative review chain, expert triage, and formal institutional export. | Reviewer triage status (`pending`, `reviewed`, `referred`, `archived`), confidence score, official dossier PDF/JSON-LD. | Provides ASI and district collectors with actionable, uncorrupted evidence. |

